<list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
  <item>Here are some equations. Find the solution to each equation using what you know about exponent rules. Be prepared to explain your reasoning.</item>
</list>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\(9^x\cdot9^7=9^7\)</para>
      </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>0</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\(\frac{9^{12}}{9^x}=9^{12}\)</para>
      </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>0</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
  <item>Answer the following questions.</item>
</list>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What is the value of \(5^0\)?</para>
      </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>For positive whole numbers, \(5^a\cdot5^b=5^{a+b}\). For this law to still hold with 0 as an exponent, \(5^0\) needs to be equal to 1 because \(5^{0+4}=5^4\) and \(1\cdot5^4=5^4\).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What about \(2^0\)?</para>
      </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>For positive whole numbers, \(2^a\cdot2^b=2^{a+b}\). For this law to still hold with 0 as an exponent, \(2^0\) needs to be equal to 1 because \(2^{0+4}=2^4\) and \(1\cdot2^4=2^4\).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    
    
  
  
  
  </problem></exercise>
<!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
<exercise class="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
    
    <para>We know, for example, that \((2+3)+5=2+(3+5)\) and \(2\cdot\left(3\cdot 5\right)=\left(2\cdot 3\right)\cdot 5\). The grouping with parentheses does not affect the value of the expression.</para>
    
    <para>Is this true for exponents? That is, are the numbers \(2^{\left(3^5\right)}\) and \(\left(2^3\right)^5\) equal? If not, which is bigger?</para>
      
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>The two numbers are not even close! Since \(3^5=243\), the number \(2^{\left(3^5\right)}\) is \(2^{243}\). On the other hand, \(\left(2^3\right)^5=2^3\cdot2^3\cdot2^3\cdot2^3\cdot2^3=2^{15}\). The number \(2^{243}\) is much larger than \(2^{15}\).</para>
    
    <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
    </list>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction-->

<exercise class="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <para>Which of the two would you choose as the meaning of the expression \(2^{3^5}\) written without parentheses?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Typically, we define repeated exponents by working down the tower, e.g., \(2^{3^5}=2^{\left(3^5\right)}\). One reason for this is that evaluating up the tower, as in \(\left(2^3\right)^5\), can already be simplified by using algebra rules (in this case, to get \(2^{15}\)).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction--></section>

<note class="self-check"><para>Santos and Sara are checking their work on the following problem: \(4\left(2\cdot3\cdot5\right)^0\).</para>
  <para>Santos says the answer is 1. Sara says the answer is 4. Who is correct, and why?</para></note>
  <section>
    <title>Additional Resources</title>
    
  <section><title>Zero as an Exponent</title>
    <para>Why is \(2^0=1\) and \(5^0=1\), but \(2^3\) is not the same as \(5^3\)?</para>
    <para>Answering this question will help us understand the <term class="glossary-tooltip">zero exponent rule</term>.</para>
    <para>Exponents in general represent repeated multiplication, much like multiplication represents repeated addition. Let’s use that understanding to evaluate \(2^3\) and \(5^3\):</para>
    <para>\(2^3=2\cdot2\cdot2=4\cdot2=8\)</para>
    <para>\(5^3=5\cdot5\cdot5=25\cdot5=125\)</para>
    <para>8 and 125 are obviously very different, but the method to get to each answer was the same. If we rewrite it as a table, we can work backward to understand how \(2^0\) and \(5^0\) are both 1.</para>
    <para>Using \(y=2^x\) and filling in the parts we have found results in Table 1:</para>
    
    <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
      <tgroup cols="5"><tbody><row>
          <entry><emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis> </entry><entry> 0 </entry>
          <entry> 1 </entry>
          <entry> 2 </entry>
          <entry> 3 </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry><emphasis>\(y\)</emphasis> </entry><entry> </entry>
          <entry> 2 </entry>
          <entry> 4 </entry>
          <entry> 8 </entry>
        </row>
      </tbody></tgroup></table>
    
    <para>Repeating that process for \(y=5^x\) results in Table 2:</para>
    
    <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
      <tgroup cols="5"><tbody><row>
          <entry><emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis> </entry><entry> 0 </entry>
          <entry> 1 </entry>
          <entry> 2 </entry>
          <entry> 3 </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry><emphasis>\(y\)</emphasis> </entry><entry> </entry>
          <entry> 5 </entry>
          <entry> 25 </entry>
          <entry> 125 </entry>
        </row>
      </tbody></tgroup></table>
    
    <para>Since exponents represent repeated multiplication in both tables, additional terms are found by multiplying again by the base. What if we worked backward? If you multiply to move to the right in the table, you can divide by the common ratio to work backward. Start with the 8, and work backward to fill in the table:</para>
    
    <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
      <tgroup cols="5"><tbody><row>
          <entry><emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis> </entry><entry> 0 </entry>
          <entry> 1 </entry>
          <entry> 2 </entry>
          <entry> 3 </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry><emphasis>\(y\)</emphasis> </entry><entry> \(2\div2=1\) </entry>
          <entry> \(4\div2=2\) </entry>
          <entry> \(8\div2=4\) </entry>
          <entry> 8 </entry>
        </row>
      </tbody></tgroup></table>
    
    <para>Repeat the process for Table 2:</para>
    
    <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
      <tgroup cols="5"><tbody><row>
          <entry><emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis> </entry><entry> 0 </entry>
          <entry> 1 </entry>
          <entry> 2 </entry>
          <entry> 3 </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry><emphasis>\(y\)</emphasis> </entry><entry> \(5\div5=1\) </entry>
          <entry> \(25\div5=5\) </entry>
          <entry> \(125\div5=25\) </entry>
          <entry> 125 </entry>
        </row>
      </tbody></tgroup></table>
    
    <para>After you have worked backward in each table, you will see that \(2^0\) and \(5^0\) both equal 1. When 1 is the exponent, your answer is the same as the common ratio, so when you divide by the common ratio, you end up with 1 as your answer every time. This is an important rule that can save a lot of time.</para>
    <note class="try look-at-me"/>
    <title>Try It: Zero as an Exponent</title>
    <exercise>
      <problem>
        
        <para>Find the value of each of the following:</para>
      
      <para>\(3^0\)</para>
          <para>\(1,246^0\)</para>
          <para>\(
            \left(2\cdot3\cdot7\right)^0\)</para>
        <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
      
      </problem><solution>
      <para>Compare your answer:</para>
      
      <para>Here is how to use the zero exponent rule to determine the value of each:</para>
      
      <para>The answer to each expression is 1. If you want to work backward, it doesn’t matter how large the original number is. \(3\div3=1\) and \(1246\div1246=1\), and both expressions only contain a base and zero as exponent, so the answer is 1. For c, there is an expression inside the parentheses that you can evaluate first, but since it is still the base raised to a zero exponent, the answer will still be one.</para>
    
    </solution></exercise>
    </section>
  </section>
  
